{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Jury theorems assume a shared, independent signal of the truth; panelists may have correlated training biases that break independence.","Checklists are artificially constrained to explicit features, unlike jury models that allow gestalt intuition.","Jury models typically assume voters share competence; non-experts may have highly variable base competence."],"overturn_conditions":["If one observes high inter-panel correlation on errors that the expert avoids, the conditional independence assumption is false.","If the expert outperforms the panel on items where the checklist aligns with the expert's explicit reasoning, the reliability gain is an artifact of task simplicity, not a general principle.","If increased panel size does not reduce variance, the ensemble mechanism is absent."],"shared_structure":["Both involve a structured decision rule and a committee of independent voters whose collective judgment aims to capture an objective outcome.","Both use conditional independence of voter errors given the decision rule to drive reliable aggregation.","In both, the completeness of the explicit rule relative to the target's generative structure determines success."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_001","SRC_006"],"transfer_claims":["The jury theorem implies that if conditional independence holds and average panelist competence exceeds a threshold, panel reliability approaches perfect accuracy as panel size grows.","Just as juries can fail when voters share a common blind spot (e.g., a biased heuristic), checklist panels will systematically co-fail on items requiring unlisted features.","The completeness of the checklist is analogous to the voters' access to the true signal; an incomplete checklist creates a systematic bias that aggregation cannot remove."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The covering principle is that panel reliability strictly dominates expert intuition whenever (i) the checklist enumerates the complete set of features on which the objectively verifiable outcome depends, and (ii) the non-experts' conditionally independent votes collectively ensemble-average away residual classification noise. Within a single evaluation, the distinguishing evidence is a pattern: high consensus panels systematically err on the same specific items where the expert succeeds—showing that an unlisted cue drives the outcome; if no such systematic co-failure occurs, the gain is genuine.","counterconditions":["If the checklist omits outcome-relevant features that expert intuition captures, the expert will systematically outperform on items requiring those features, falsifying panel superiority.","If panel members' votes are conditionally dependent (e.g., through shared training biases), ensemble averaging does not reduce residual noise, undermining the reliability mechanism.","If the task's feature space is too complex for explicit enumeration, the checklist cannot serve as a complete decision rule, and the covering principle collapses.","If the expert's calibration is high and the task distribution is narrow, the stochastic advantage of the ensemble may be negligible.","If the objective outcome is sensitive to a continuously interacting threshold that a discrete checklist cannot approximate, the mechanism fails."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Expert intuition tacitly encodes feature-use cues through fast-and-frugal pattern matching but suffers from inconsistent retrieval, anchoring, and attention lapses. The checklist substitutes a deterministic decision rule, while the panel acts as a bagging-style ensemble. Conditional on the checklist, the only residual variance is classification noise; independence ensures aggregation drives this noise toward a reliable expected signal.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","824b622317becd42defa50f5d17bcfb519cceef3f5eef22740cd5a634ee24b7b"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.92},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Reading comprehension involves understanding an existing text; panel judgment involves applying a rule to new data.","The perturbation in reading tests is textual; in panels, it is the decision rule itself.","Understanding a text and understanding a checklist's causal role in an outcome are different cognitive tasks."],"overturn_conditions":["If the panel maintains high performance under rephrasing, this supports the understanding residual and the reliability gain.","If the panel fails under rephrasing but the expert also fails under analogous perturbation, the distinction is moot.","If the rephrased checklist is objectively harder to apply (e.g., more ambiguous), a performance drop does not prove mere compliance."],"shared_structure":["Both involve a distinction between syntactic matching (compliance) and semantic abstraction (understanding).","Both predict that a true grasp of the underlying structure should be invariant to surface form.","Both imply that performance dependent on surface form is a sign of a fragile, non-generalizable mechanism."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_005"],"transfer_claims":["If panelists genuinely understand the checklist, their accuracy should be invariant to rephrasing, just as reading comprehension is invariant to paraphrase.","A collapse in performance under rephrasing indicates the panel was relying on surface features (compliance) rather than deep features (understanding), just as poor performance on paraphrased texts indicates shallow reading.","The presence of an understanding residual in the panelist's cognitive model is what distinguishes a robust reliability gain from a brittle compliance artifact."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The distinguishing mark of mere checklist compliance is that panel performance will catastrophically collapse when the checklist is reordered or rephrased, while a genuine reliability gain reflects a deep understanding residual that is invariant to the form of the checklist. A panel following an explicit checklist will, on average, produce more reliable judgments because the checklist acts as an externalized cognitive scaffold, but this is true only if panelists possess an understanding residual that survives formal reordering.","counterconditions":["If the rephrased checklist is functionally equivalent but panel performance collapses, the original gain was mere compliance, and the covering principle is false for that panel.","If no understanding residual exists (performance is entirely explained by the checklist score), the panel cannot adapt to any distributional shift.","If the reordering is so extreme that it breaks the semantic coherence of the checklist, even understanding panelists will fail, making this a false negative.","If the expert's intuition is actually a form of implicit checklist-following, the distinction between compliance and understanding is illusory."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Mere compliance tracks the right answer by superficial lexical or sequential matching to a trained template. Genuine reliability gain stems from a causal understanding of the checklist's items that allows panelists to reweight or reorder features mentally. If panelists rely solely on compliance, their performance is brittle; if they possess an understanding residual, their performance is robust to perturbation.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["54bff7243e3549f4d613685198b47e34ae41723ff159dc12bb1e66f75cba7e48"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.88},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["In causal learning, the residual is an individual's learning; in panels, it is a collective property.","The checklist is an external artifact; the direct instruction is an experimental manipulation.","The outcome in causal learning is a model's internal state; in panels, it is an externally verifiable judgment."],"overturn_conditions":["If the understanding residual is non-significant, the panel is merely a compliance engine, and the expert's intuition is superior if it captures the unlisted features.","If the residual is fully explained by individual panelist competence rather than collective interaction, the 'panel' superiority is just a sampling artifact of picking good individuals.","If the expert's intuition is also modeled and its residual is larger than the panel's, the expert wins."],"shared_structure":["Both involve isolating a residual effect after controlling for a primary, explicit pathway.","Both use statistical mediation to distinguish a superficial mechanism (checklist compliance, explicit instruction) from a deeper mechanism (understanding residual, direct learning).","Both require that the residual is causally relevant to the outcome, not just statistically correlated."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_006"],"transfer_claims":["Just as a significant residual effect after instruction shows that learning occurred beyond the explicit prompt, a significant panel-specific residual after checklist scoring shows that panel understanding exists beyond mere compliance.","If the panel's residual mediates the outcome, the panel is not just a checklist-executor but a genuine reasoning agent.","The method of partitioning variance into explicit-rule and residual-understanding components transfers directly."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The panel's reliability gain is genuine if and only if a mediator model of the outcome reveals a significant understanding residual—panel-specific cognitive insight—after controlling for the checklist score. The claim that panels outperform experts is true only when the panel's collective judgment captures features of the outcome that the explicit checklist alone does not, implying a form of distributed collective intelligence.","counterconditions":["If the understanding residual is zero or fully explained by the checklist score, the panel's performance is mere compliance, and the claim of panel superiority is an artifact of the checklist's design.","If the expert's intuition is fully captured by the checklist, there is no understanding residual for either to exploit.","If the panel's residual is actually noise that happens to correlate with the outcome in the evaluation sample, the model overfits.","If the understanding operates entirely through the checklist, the mediator model is misspecified."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The outcome is a function of both the checklist's explicit features and an unlisted, tacit feature set. The expert captures the tacit set intuitively but unreliably. The panel, through deliberation or aggregation, synthesizes a collective understanding that captures the tacit set more reliably than a single expert. This is operationalized as: outcome ~ checklist_score + panel_understanding_residual. If the residual is significant and panel-correlated, the gain is genuine.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["824b622317becd42defa50f5d17bcfb519cceef3f5eef22740cd5a634ee24b7b"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.82},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Checklists are not necessarily diagnostic; they could be generative or heuristic.","Non-expert panels can aggregate diverse perspectives, potentially accessing more features than a single expert.","The outcome in clinical judgment is often subjective; here it is objectively verifiable."],"overturn_conditions":["If the panel outperforms the expert on items requiring tacit features, the expert's intuition is not superior, and the claim is false.","If the checklist can be dynamically updated to include new features, the panel's boundedness is temporary.","If the expert's 'tacit' features are actually just noise or bias, the expert's success on those items is spurious."],"shared_structure":["Both involve a conflict between an explicit, rule-based system and an intuitive, holistic system.","Both assume the intuitive system captures features the explicit system cannot.","Both predict the intuitive system wins on complex, high-cue tasks and loses on simple, measurable tasks."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_008"],"transfer_claims":["Just as clinical algorithmic models fail to capture holistic case features that experts use, checklist panels fail to capture tacit outcome features.","The systematic co-failure of the panel on items where the expert succeeds is analogous to the statistical model's failure on outlier clinical cases.","The distinction between the explicit checklist's feature space and the expert's tacit feature space maps to the actuarial vs. clinical judgment divide."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The claim is generally false: a trained non-expert panel with a checklist will not reliably outperform a single domain expert relying on intuition, because the expert's tacit feature recognition operates on a richer, higher-dimensional feature space than any explicit checklist can encode. The single evaluation can distinguish mere compliance from a true gain by testing for systematic co-failures: panels will systematically err on items requiring tacit cues the checklist omits, whereas experts will succeed on those items, proving the panel's performance is checklist-bounded.","counterconditions":["If the checklist is genuinely complete for the task domain, the expert's tacit knowledge adds no value, and the panel's structured process may win.","If the panel's deliberation allows them to collectively infer tacit cues not listed, they are not bound by the checklist's explicit limit.","If the expert's intuition is poorly calibrated or biased, the tacit features may be misused, and the structured panel may outperform despite the feature deficit.","If the task is simple and low-dimensional, the checklist may capture all relevant features, making the expert's advantage negligible."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Expert intuition is a form of sophisticated pattern recognition honed by experience, capable of integrating weak, implicit, and interacting cues that resist explicit codification. A checklist, by definition, restricts the decision to a pre-specified, low-dimensional subset of features. The panel cannot access the omitted features, so their accuracy is bounded by the checklist's coverage. When the outcome depends on an omitted cue, the panel will systematically fail while the expert succeeds.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["e4202e40887c4a597211a812b3707fc0a88609e1b332ba52b4190435366bd9d7"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.78},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Visual perception is low-level and automatic; expert judgment is high-level and effortful.","The focal vs. global distinction in perception is spatial; in judgment, it is feature-integration.","The checkerboard task is instantaneous; panel judgment is deliberative."],"overturn_conditions":["If the panel outperforms on holistic items, the checklist does not impair integration, or the panel is using a holistic process.","If the expert outperforms on decomposable items, the expert's intuition is more reliable than the panel's aggregation, perhaps due to superior calibration.","If there is no crossover interaction, the task is uniformly on one end of the continuum, and the principle is not tested."],"shared_structure":["Both involve a trade-off between structured, piecemeal processing and holistic, gestalt processing.","Both predict that performance depends on the match between the processing mode and the task's underlying structure.","Both imply that forcing a structure (checklist, analytic focus) on a holistic task impairs performance."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_003"],"transfer_claims":["Just as a narrow spotlight impairs recognition of a global visual gestalt, a rigid checklist impairs recognition of a holistic outcome pattern.","Panel performance will show a crossover interaction: superior on decomposed items, inferior on integrated items.","The distinction between compliance and genuine gain is task-dependent: compliance is sufficient for decomposable tasks, but genuine understanding is needed for holistic ones."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The covering principle is that neither approach is universally superior; reliability depends on the underlying task structure's amenability to decomposition. Panels with checklists dominate on decomposable tasks where the outcome is a function of explicitly enumerable features, while expert intuition dominates on holistic tasks where the outcome depends on gestalt, interacting features. The distinguishing evidence within a single evaluation is a crossover interaction: panels will outperform on items matching the checklist's features but underperform on items requiring feature integration beyond the checklist.","counterconditions":["If the task is fully decomposable, the panel always wins, and the expert offers no advantage.","If the task is fully holistic, the expert always wins, and the checklist is a hindrance.","If the panel can deliberate and integrate features holistically, they are not purely following the checklist, muddying the comparison.","If the expert can decompose their intuition into a checklist, they can become a panel of one, equalizing the advantage."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Decomposable tasks allow the checklist to serve as a sufficient statistic for the outcome. The panel's value is in reliably computing this statistic through aggregation. Holistic tasks require feature integration that a sequential, explicit checklist disrupts. The expert's value is in parallel, holistic processing. Thus, the relative reliability is a function of the task's position on a decomposition-holism continuum.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The critical, atypical covering principle is that the objectivity of the outcome metric itself can create a 'compliance mirage': a panel may appear more reliable simply because the objective outcome was defined in terms of the same explicit features the checklist tracks, artificially favoring the panel's methodology. Genuine reliability gain is only distinguished by testing the panel against an orthogonal, held-out objective outcome that the checklist was not designed to track. If the panel fails on the orthogonal outcome, the original gain was mere compliance to a narrow target.","counterconditions":["If the held-out orthogonal outcome is invalid or measures a different construct, the test is unfair.","If the expert's intuition also fails on the orthogonal outcome, neither approach generalizes, and the original comparison is void.","If the checklist is updated to track the new outcome, the distinction is lost.","If the objective outcome is uniquely correct and the orthogonal outcome is merely noise, the expert's failure on the primary metric is a true deficit, not a metric artifact."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The construction of the objectively verifiable outcome is not neutral. If the outcome metric is defined by a set of explicit criteria, and the checklist is also built on those criteria, the panel is tautologically favored. The expert's intuition, which may track a richer, real-world signal, is penalized for not matching the narrow metric. A genuine reliability gain should generalize to related but differently-measured outcomes. If it does not, the panel is merely compliant with the specific metric.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3","ec610dd2a99346cda259e809f4594423eddbc6c51591e57d1f87b4e33722baac"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.45}],"context_request":{"desired_retrieval_channels":[],"requested_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","824b622317becd42defa50f5d17bcfb519cceef3f5eef22740cd5a634ee24b7b","54bff7243e3549f4d613685198b47e34ae41723ff159dc12bb1e66f75cba7e48","e4202e40887c4a597211a812b3707fc0a88609e1b332ba52b4190435366bd9d7","30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3","ec610dd2a99346cda259e809f4594423eddbc6c51591e57d1f87b4e33722baac"]},"simulation_proposals":[]}